# compiler-absent-from-shipped-binary — the clautolisp executable had no compiler

STATUS: DONE in 2.0.12. Found 2026-08-28 while adding the --optimize CLI
option, by measuring what the option actually did; fixed the same day.

## Symptom

The `clautolisp` executable contained no compiler. Every SPEED level
behaved like SPEED 0: `-O0` and `-O3` produced byte-identical behaviour
and indistinguishable run times.

Measured on the built SBCL image, `(defun fib …) (princ (fib 27))`,
best of repeated runs:

    before the fix          after the fix
      -O0   851 ms            -O0   916 ms
      -O2   845 ms            -O2   734 ms
      -O3   858 ms            -O3   747 ms
      ratio 1.00              ratio 1.24

The `before` column is the bug in one line: asking for optimization and
asking for none took the same time, because neither compiled anything.
(The two columns are different images and their absolute numbers are not
comparable; the ratio WITHIN a column is the measurement.)

## Repro

Before the fix, with the built executable:

    clautolisp -O0 -l fib.lsp
    clautolisp -O3 -l fib.lsp

Same time, to the millisecond.

## Cause

`clautolisp/clautolisp-tool` did not depend on
`clautolisp/autolisp-compiler`, so the compiler was never loaded into the
image, so its `(setf *compile-usubr-hook* …)` toplevel forms never ran, so
every hook the runtime consults to reach the compiler stayed NIL — and a
NIL hook means `maybe-compile-usubr` returns NIL and the interpreter runs.
No error, no warning, no diagnostic: the runtime is *designed* to work with
those hooks unset, because that is exactly the configuration in which the
compiler has not been loaded.

The dependency inversion that makes the compiler optional is deliberate and
correct — the runtime and the builtins reach the compiler only through
hooks, so neither depends on it. What was missing is its consequence:
because nothing below pulls the compiler in, the program that assembles the
components has to name it explicitly, and only the test systems did.

## Why ten slices of green tests never caught it

Every compiler test system depends on `clautolisp/autolisp-compiler`
directly, which is what a test of the compiler must do. So the hooks were
always installed *in the test image*, and the tests were true — of the test
image. Nothing in the corpus ever asked the question the user asks, which
is whether the program on disk has a compiler in it.

This is the same shape of mistake as the CCL failures in 2.0.11, one level
up: there the tests ran on one host and were reported as if they held for
both; here they ran in one image and were reported as if they held for the
program. In both cases the test was not wrong, the extrapolation was.

## Fix

Add `"clautolisp/autolisp-compiler"` to the `:depends-on` of
`clautolisp/clautolisp-tool` in clautolisp.asd, with a comment recording
why it cannot be inherited from anything below it.

## The gate

Fixing the dependency does not stop it recurring, so the same commit adds
one: `make test-shipped-compiler`, which builds the executable and runs
tools/clautolisp/tests/shipped-compiler-test.sh against it. Three checks:
CLAL-COMPILE-FILE produces an artefact, the .lap loads and the function it
defines runs, and `-O speed=0,debug=1` is what (clal-optimization) reports.

The probe is CLAL-COMPILE-FILE rather than a timing comparison because it
is the one surface that distinguishes "no compiler in this build" from
every other failure: it signals COMPILER-NOT-AVAILABLE instead of quietly
falling back. Timing would test the same thing less reliably.

The gate was verified RED before being believed: with the dependency
removed and the executable rebuilt, it fails on all three .lap checks and
names the missing line in clautolisp.asd in its output. (The -O check
still passes there, correctly -- the option plumbing does not depend on
the compiler being present.) A gate never seen red is not a gate, which
is the same mistake in miniature as the one this ticket is about.

Wired into both CI lanes (GitLab test:clautolisp:compiler:sbcl, mirrored
into .github/workflows/ci.yml), together with test-compiler-equivalence
and test-debug-compiled -- which, it turned out, had never been in CI
either.
